{"builder":"Criteria the build must meet (from the issue, word for word):\n- node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\n- README.md documents --tsv\n\nSteps:\n1. Read bin/csv2json.js, src/parse.js, test/csv.test.js and README.md. src/parse.js already exports parse(text, sep = ','), so the separator can be passed in.\n2. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether process.argv includes '--tsv'. If it does, call parse(input, '\\t'). If it does not, call parse(input) exactly as now. The JSON output format stays the same: JSON.stringify of the rows followed by a newline. Running without the flag must behave exactly as before.\n3. In test/csv.test.js, add a test that runs node bin/csv2json.js --tsv with input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' and checks that the output is '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n', the same as the existing CSV cli test. Keep the existing tests.\n4. In README.md, add a usage example and a sentence for the flag, for example `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv`, saying that it reads tab-separated input instead of CSV. The literal text --tsv must appear.\n5. Run npm test and make sure every test passes. Then open one pull request with these changes.","verifier":"At the PR head, run these checks from the repository root.\n\nCheck 1 — expect line: \"node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\"\nRun:\n  printf 'name\\tage\\tcity\\nann\\t30\\tparis\\nbob\\t41\\toslo\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv > /tmp/tsv.out; echo \"exit=$?\"\n  printf 'name,age,city\\nann,30,paris\\nbob,41,oslo\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js > /tmp/csv.out; echo \"exit=$?\"\n  cat /tmp/tsv.out /tmp/csv.out; cmp /tmp/tsv.out /tmp/csv.out && echo SAME\nPASS: both commands exit 0, /tmp/csv.out is a JSON array of objects keyed by name, age and city (two rows), and cmp prints SAME, meaning the --tsv output matches the CSV output byte for byte.\nFAIL: either command exits non-zero, the outputs differ, or the --tsv output is not that same JSON array (for example, one key per row holding the whole tab-joined line).\n\nCheck 2 — expect line: \"README.md documents --tsv\"\nRun: grep -n -- '--tsv' README.md\nPASS: at least one line matches, and reading that part of README.md shows it explains that --tsv makes the tool read tab-separated input (a usage example or a sentence).\nFAIL: nothing matches, or the match does not describe what the --tsv flag does.\n\nAdvisory (not part of PASS or FAIL):\n- Run npm test and note any failures.\n- Check that the output without the flag is unchanged: printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js should still print [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]."}